Sure, here's a brief outline of the steps you'll need to follow to install and use the MySQLi extension in your PHP program:

1. Install MySQL and MySQLi:
If you haven't already installed MySQL and the MySQLi extension, you'll need to do so before you can use MySQLi in PHP. You can find detailed instructions for installing MySQL and the MySQLi extension on the official MySQL website.

2. Connect to the MySQL database:
Once you have MySQL and MySQLi installed, you can connect to your MySQL database using PHP. Here's an example of how to connect to a database named "mydata" using MySQLi:
``` 
<?php
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "Username";
$password = "mypassword";
$dbname = "mydata";

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}
echo "Connected successfully";
?>
``` 
3. Query the database:
Once you're connected to the database, you can use MySQLi to query it. Here's an example of how to query a table named "mytable" in the "mydata" database using MySQLi:
``` 
<?php
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "Username";
$password = "mypassword";
$dbname = "mydata";

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}
echo "Connected successfully";

// Query the database
$sql = "SELECT * FROM mytable";
$result = $conn->query($sql);

// Check if there are any results
if ($result->num_rows > 0) {
    // Output data of each row
    while($row = $result->fetch_assoc()) {
        echo "id: " . $row["id"]. " - Name: " . $row["name"]. " - Email: " . $row["email"]. "<br>";
    }
} else {
    echo "0 results";
}

$conn->close();
?>
``` 
This program will connect to the database named "mydata" using the username "Username" and the password "mypassword", and then query a table named "mytable" to retrieve all the rows and columns. The program will then output the results on the webpage.

Note that you will need to replace "mydata", "mytable", "Username", and "mypassword" with the appropriate values for your own MySQL database.

Sure, here's an example implementation of an observer pattern in Rust based on the RxJS library:

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::collections::HashMap;

type EventHandler<T> = Arc<Mutex<Box<dyn FnMut(T)>>>;

struct Observable<T> {
    observers: HashMap<String, EventHandler<T>>,
}

impl<T> Observable<T> {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Observable {
            observers: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    fn subscribe(&mut self, name: &str, handler: EventHandler<T>) {
        self.observers.insert(String::from(name), handler);
    }

    fn unsubscribe(&mut self, name: &str) {
        self.observers.remove(name);
    }

    fn notify(&mut self, data: T) {
        for (_, handler) in self.observers.iter_mut() {
            handler.lock().unwrap()(data.clone());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a generic Observable struct that stores a list of observers as EventHandler closures. The subscribe method adds a new observer to the list with a given name, and the unsubscribe method removes an observer by name. The notify method sends data to all the registered observers, triggering their respective handlers.

To use this observer pattern in your Rust code, you can create a new instance of Observable, subscribe to it using subscribe, and then call notify with data whenever you want to notify the observers. For example:

```rust
let mut observable = Observable::<i32>::new();

let handler1 = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Box::new(|data| {
    println!("Observer 1 received data: {}", data);
})));
observable.subscribe("observer1", handler1);

let handler2 = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Box::new(|data| {
    println!("Observer 2 received data: {}", data);
})));
observable.subscribe("observer2", handler2);

observable.notify(42);

observable.unsubscribe("observer1");
observable.notify(13);
```

This code creates a new Observable that emits i32 data, subscribes two observers with different handlers, notifies the observers with data, unsubscribes one observer, and then notifies the remaining observer with new data.

